WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.422
By the end of this module, you should be able to choose a join based on the business question rather

00:00:07.422 --> 00:00:10.377
than simply choosing the join type you remember first.

00:00:10.377 --> 00:00:18.527
For example, the phrase only patients who have admissions suggests a different join from every patient,

00:00:18.527 --> 00:00:21.072
whether or not an admission exists.

00:00:21.072 --> 00:00:23.297
You will also practice using aliases, ON conditions, and USING.

00:00:23.297 --> 00:00:29.082
Aliases are especially important when several tables contain columns with similar names.

00:00:29.082 --> 00:00:36.152
They help us state clearly which table a column comes from and make longer SQL queries easier to read.

00:00:36.152 --> 00:00:40.052
A major goal is understanding grain and cardinality.

00:00:40.052 --> 00:00:42.777
Grain means what one row represents.

00:00:42.777 --> 00:00:49.367
One row could represent one patient, one admission, one lab test, or one department.

00:00:49.367 --> 00:00:55.222
Cardinality describes how many rows on one side can match rows on the other side.

00:00:55.222 --> 00:01:03.512
One patient may match several admissions, and several one-to-many relationships can cause row multiplication

00:01:03.512 --> 00:01:04.467
if we are not careful.

00:01:04.467 --> 00:01:09.237
Finally, you should be able to validate a joined dataset.

00:01:09.237 --> 00:01:15.192
We will compare row counts, count distinct keys, inspect known examples, and check unmatched cases.

00:01:15.192 --> 00:01:20.692
A query that runs without an error is not automatically a correct analytical result.
